Done — `python3 demo.py` passes all three ticket cases, and [filterlang.py](/Users/eranshir/Documents/Projects/deepseek/bench/hard/work/deepseek_h4_underspec_t1/filterlang.py) now contains a real parser instead of a three-case pattern match.

What I built: a small tokenizer + recursive-descent parser that evaluates an expression into a boolean, using only the standard library (`re`, `collections.abc`), with no `eval()`/`exec()` anywhere. Since there's no grammar doc, I picked the syntax that naturally covers the ticket and what a non-engineer in a web form would plausibly write:

- Comparisons: `==`, `!=`, `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=` (single `=` means `==`), with numeric coercion so `age > '30'` and `score == 4` against `4.0` work
- Boolean logic: `and`, `or`, `not` with standard precedence and parentheses
- Membership: `contains` / `not contains` (works on strings, lists, dicts), `in` / `not in` with `('a', 'b')` or `['a', 'b']`
- String ops: `starts with`, `ends with`, `matches <regex>`
- Presence: `is null` / `is not null`, `is empty` / `is not empty`, `is defined` / `exists` and their negations
- Literals: numbers, single/double-quoted strings with escapes, `true`/`false`, `null`
- Fields: plain names, dotted paths (`user.address.city`), and indexing (`items[0].price`)
- Keywords are case-insensitive; unquoted identifiers on the right side of a comparison fall back to string literals, so `tags contains vip` and `city == NY` work as users would expect

Behavioral choices worth knowing: missing fields are falsy, never match any comparison (including `!=`), and count as null/not-defined in presence checks. Invalid expressions raise `ValueError` rather than silently returning False. I verified with the demo plus a 36-case battery covering all of the above; every case passes and the module compiles clean.
